  "worked_solution": "Bringing all the terms to the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\ge 49 \\\\ {x}^{2}-49 & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y={x}^{2}-49\\,$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-49 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{7}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-7)(x+7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x=\\pm 7.$ \n\nSolving the inequality ${x}^{2}-49\\ge 0$ is equivalent to finding the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y={x}^{2}-49$ is above the $x$-axis or intersects the $x$-axis (where $y\\ge 0$).\n\nSince the leading coefficient of the quadratic expression is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution set lies outside the roots (including them), as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/5993bb434fca17beb9842d7963c5f560b4d8ef0bacadea4c120160d2ab93d131.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup [7,\\infty ).$",
